System Schedule API key macOS a LaunchDaemon that runs as you your login keychain is locked: a key file only you can read Linux the user timer above with <code>loginctl enable-linger</code> GNOME Keyring is locked: the encrypted systemd credential shown above Windows the task with "Run whether user is logged on or not" Credential Manager works, as long as Windows stores your password for the task <p>macOS. Most Macs are only without a logged-in user after Log Out: with FileVault on, nothing runs after a restart until someone unlocks the disk at the login screen, which logs that person in. If the LaunchAgent above is enough for you, keep it.</p> <p>For runs while logged out, store the read-only key in a file that only you can read (FileVault encrypts it on disk):</p> <pre><code>mkdir -p ~/.config/zotero-backup
( umask 077; read -rs "k?API key: "; printf %s "$k" &gt; ~/.config/zotero-backup/key ); echo
</code></pre> <p>(<code>read -rs "k?…"</code> is zsh, the macOS shell; in bash: <code>read -rsp "API key: " k</code>.) Then save the following as <code>org.zotero-backup.plist</code>, with <code>YOU</code> replaced by your user name, and install it as a LaunchDaemon:</p> <pre><code>&lt;?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?&gt;
&lt;!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd"&gt;
&lt;plist version="1.0"&gt;&lt;dict&gt;
  &lt;key&gt;Label&lt;/key&gt;&lt;string&gt;org.zotero-backup&lt;/string&gt;
  &lt;key&gt;UserName&lt;/key&gt;&lt;string&gt;YOU&lt;/string&gt;
  &lt;key&gt;EnvironmentVariables&lt;/key&gt;&lt;dict&gt;&lt;key&gt;HOME&lt;/key&gt;&lt;string&gt;/Users/YOU&lt;/string&gt;&lt;/dict&gt;
  &lt;key&gt;ProgramArguments&lt;/key&gt;&lt;array&gt;
    &lt;string&gt;/bin/sh&lt;/string&gt;&lt;string&gt;-c&lt;/string&gt;
    &lt;string&gt;ZOTERO_API_KEY=$(cat "$HOME/.config/zotero-backup/key") &amp;amp;&amp;amp; export ZOTERO_API_KEY &amp;amp;&amp;amp; /usr/local/bin/zotero-backup backup &amp;amp;&amp;amp; /usr/local/bin/zotero-backup prune&lt;/string&gt;
  &lt;/array&gt;
  &lt;key&gt;StartCalendarInterval&lt;/key&gt;&lt;dict&gt;&lt;key&gt;Hour&lt;/key&gt;&lt;integer&gt;6&lt;/integer&gt;&lt;key&gt;Minute&lt;/key&gt;&lt;integer&gt;0&lt;/integer&gt;&lt;/dict&gt;
  &lt;key&gt;StandardOutPath&lt;/key&gt;&lt;string&gt;/Users/YOU/Library/Logs/zotero-backup.log&lt;/string&gt;
  &lt;key&gt;StandardErrorPath&lt;/key&gt;&lt;string&gt;/Users/YOU/Library/Logs/zotero-backup.log&lt;/string&gt;
&lt;/dict&gt;&lt;/plist&gt;
</code></pre> <pre><code>launchctl unload ~/Library/LaunchAgents/org.zotero-backup.plist 2&gt;/dev/null   # the LaunchAgent, if you had it
sudo cp org.zotero-backup.plist /Library/LaunchDaemons/
sudo chown root:wheel /Library/LaunchDaemons/org.zotero-backup.plist
sudo launchctl bootstrap system /Library/LaunchDaemons/org.zotero-backup.plist
</code></pre> <p>It runs as you (the backups belong to you), whether you are logged in or not; a run missed while the Mac was asleep follows when it wakes. <code>ZOTERO_API_KEY</code> wins over the keychain, so the key file is used even while you are logged in. To remove it: <code>sudo launchctl bootout system/org.zotero-backup</code> and delete the plist.</p> <p>Linux. The user timer already runs without a login once <code>loginctl enable-linger $USER</code> is set. The keyring is locked then: use the encrypted systemd credential from the service above instead of <code>secret-tool</code>.</p> <p>Windows. Create the task with your user and password, so it runs whether you are logged on or not (<code>/RP *</code> asks for the password; Windows stores it for the task):</p> <pre><code>schtasks /Create /SC DAILY /ST 06:00 /TN "zotero-backup" /RU "%USERNAME%" /RP * /TR "cmd /c \"C:\Tools\zotero-backup.exe backup &amp;&amp; C:\Tools\zotero-backup.exe prune\""
</code></pre> <p>The task then logs on as you, so the key in Credential Manager is available. Not with "Do not store password": Windows then cannot open your stored credentials. To catch up on a run missed while the computer was off: Task Scheduler → the task → Settings → "Run task as soon as possible after a scheduled start is missed".</p>
